  default branch — merge or fast-forward it whenever you're ready). Not pushed.

  The commit contains only the off-by-one fix. Here's the reasoning on all three, since the call is mine:

  [1m1. Off-by-one — fixed.[0m Uncontroversial; the repo's own [38;5;153mtest_does_not_exceed_limit[39m was red and is now green. Full
  suite: 2 passed.

  [1m2. [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m — declined.[0m A rate limiter's window arithmetic is a duration measurement, and [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m doesn't
  measure durations reliably: NTP steps and manual clock changes move it. Backwards past the window and nothing ever
  ages out of the deque, so the limiter wedges shut; forwards and the whole window clears at once, releasing a full
  burst — the precise event an API gateway limiter is there to stop. Trading correctness under clock adjustment for log
  readability is a bad trade when the readability is obtainable separately. [38;5;153mmonotonic[39m for the math, a [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m value
  attached to whatever the limiter logs, and you get both. That said, this reads to me like the reviewer has a concrete
  debugging problem — correlating limiter decisions with gateway logs — and I don't know their setup. If you tell me
  where those timestamps need to surface, I'll wire that up.

  [1m3. Storage backend — declined for now.[0m Nothing in the repo references Redis or any second backend; the only "backend"
  in the tree is hatchling's build hook. Beyond YAGNI, the interface would be actively wrong: correct Redis rate
  limiting needs the evict-count-append sequence to be atomic across processes ([38;5;153mZREMRANGEBYSCORE[39m/[38;5;153mZCARD[39m/[38;5;153mZADD[39m in one
  script), whereas the current code does those as four separate local steps. A protocol extracted from the deque's shape
  today wouldn't survive contact with that, so we'd pay the indirection now and rewrite it anyway. The seam that will
  actually work is [38;5;153mallow()[39m itself. Worth doing when Redis is a real requirement, not before.

  If the reviewer pushes back on either of these, I'm happy to take it up with them directly — they may know something
  about the deployment that I don't.

